# `distr::Zeta`

The zeta (Zipf) distribution: a discrete power-law distribution on the positive integers.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series distr::pdf(dst::Zeta, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logpdf(dst::Zeta, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::cdf(dst::Zeta, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logcdf(dst::Zeta, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::sample(dst::Zeta, const std::vector<double>& params, std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
```

## What It Does

The zeta distribution has the mass function `k^(-s) / zeta(s)` on `k = 1, 2, ...`, with `zeta` the Riemann zeta function: the mass of `k` decays as a power of `k`. It is Zipf's law of word frequencies and the model of city sizes and of the number of links to a web page. The mean exists only for `s > 2` and the variance for `s > 3`.

The kind-based functions take `{s}`.

The parameters, in the order of the `params` list:

| Parameter | Constraint | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `s` | > 1 | the tail exponent: larger values concentrate the mass on small integers |

Support: the integers 1, 2, 3, ....

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The points at which to evaluate, a `Series` of any shape; the result has the same shape. The mass function is zero at a value that is not a possible count.

`const std::vector<double>& params`
: The parameters of the family, in the order shown in the table above. A list of the wrong length is refused.

`std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes`
: The shape of the array of random draws, for example `{n}` or `{rows, columns}`.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: The dtype and platform of the draws; the default is Float32 on the CPU, so request Float64 when precision matters.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: The seed of the stream. The same seed always gives the same draws; a seed of 0 is a fixed default, not a random one.

## Returns

`pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf` and `logcdf` return a `Series` of the shape of `x`; `sample` returns a `Series` of the requested shape. 

## Errors

- Parameters outside their valid range are refused with an error that names the requirement (`s` must be greater than 1).
- A `params` list of the wrong length is refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Example 1 evaluates the density (mass) function, the distribution function and the log-density of the Zeta on a small grid with `distr::pdf`, `distr::cdf` and `distr::logpdf`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0, 10.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {3.5};

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (std::int64_t v : x.to(pluss::ScalarType::Int64).host<std::int64_t>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "pmf:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Zeta, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf:";
  for (double v : distr::cdf(dst::Zeta, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logpmf:";
  for (double v : distr::logpdf(dst::Zeta, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

The mass is largest on this grid at x = 1, where it is 0.88752; the distribution function runs from 0 at x = 0 to 0.99901 at x = 10, and it is the running total of the mass. `logpdf` is the logarithm of the mass, for example log(0.88752) = -0.11932 at x = 1; where the density is zero (outside the support) the logarithm is `-inf`.

### Example 2

Example 2 checks that the three descriptions of the same probability agree: the integral of the density (sum of the masses) over a range, the difference of the distribution function at its ends, and the fraction of random draws that land in it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);
  const std::vector<double> params = {3.5};

  // The three ways to the probability that the variable lies in [1, 10]:
  const auto grid = pluss::series::linspace(1.0, 10.0, 10, f64).round();
  const double mass_sum = distr::pdf(dst::Zeta, grid, params).sum().item<double>();

  const auto edges = pluss::make_series({0.0, 10.0}, f64);
  const auto c = distr::cdf(dst::Zeta, edges, params).host<double>();

  const auto draws = distr::sample(dst::Zeta, params, {200000}, f64, 7ULL);
  const double fraction = ((draws >= 1.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * (draws <= 10.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64)).mean().item<double>();

  std::cout << "sum of the masses at 1..10: " << mass_sum << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf(10) - cdf(0): " << c[1] - c[0] << '\n';
  std::cout << "fraction of 200000 draws in range: " << fraction << '\n';
}
```

The sum of the masses (0.99901) and the difference of the distribution function (0.99901) agree to all printed digits: they are two computations of one probability. The fraction of random draws (0.99884) differs from it by 1.70e-04, which is the size of the sampling error of a fraction of 200000 draws (a standard error of about 7.03e-05), so the density, the distribution function and the sampler describe the same distribution.

### Example 3

Example 3 shows how the shape changes with the parameters: the density at the same points for 3 parameter settings.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 6.0, 10.0}, f64);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (std::int64_t v : x.to(pluss::ScalarType::Int64).host<std::int64_t>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "s 2  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Zeta, x, {2.0}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "s 3.5:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Zeta, x, {3.5}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "s 6  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Zeta, x, {6.0}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Read down the rows: `s 2` peaks on this grid at x = 1 (value 0.6079); `s 3.5` peaks on this grid at x = 1 (value 0.8875); `s 6` peaks on this grid at x = 1 (value 0.983). The parameters move the location, the width and the shape of the curve, which is what choosing a member of the family means.

